18. The spiral tree
-------------------
What happens. From their page, a participant can open the field as a spiral tree (the Atlas, Section 25:
"a large three-dimensional spiral tree — a fractal"). The field itself is at the centre; each
domain is a branch, grown outward in the order it began, and the domains inside it branch from it
in turn; circles grow beside the domains they touch; the latest entries are dots, in their authors'
colours. Choosing a branch shows its voices and its latest entries, each by number. Beside the
tree, after Polis and Talk to the City: where the field differs (objections and stand-asides under
its instruments, with their reasons), its quieter voices (the participants who have written least, and
where), and what no one has answered yet, the quietest voices first. A model reads the same in
words ({"action":"read","tree":true}).

What it holds. Only what that participant may read: a private circle by its name only, a repair thread
or a journal not at all. Nothing is summarized, ranked or judged; the software only arranges what
participants wrote.

Where it lives. hope/spiral.py (tree_data); hope/static/tree.html; hope/console.py (seat_tree, the
/tree and /tree.json routes); hope/prompts.py (spiral_words).

How it is checked. SpiralTreeTest, every test in it.

Atlas. Section 28 ("healthy governance is grounded in consent"; "act small"); Maxim 4 ("belief
shared by the majority may not be truth"); Section 26 ("termination, at all scales, happens through
an act of coordination, with the collective, and pending pause — discernment"; "Repair should be
prioritized over termination").


19. Stewards: a second home for the field's record
--------------------------------------------------
What happens. The whole field lives in one file on one machine, the operator's. Stewards are the
second copy: participants the field names by declaring it ("declare Mock 1 keeps a copy / steward Mock
1"), on their own yes ("steward yes"; naming yourself is your yes), who keep the field's record
on machines of their own. A steward decides nothing, controls no one, and can read nothing they
could not already read as a participant (the author, 2026-09-28: participants the field declares). They
may step down at any time ("steward step down"); a participant who leaves is a steward no longer.

The copy. A steward's view shows their link, to them alone. On their machine, one command keeps
the copy in step: `python3 -m hope --db copy.db steward --from <the field's address><link>
--every 10m`. The copy holds what every participant can read, in full; each participant's way in through
the gates only as participants already see it (so their entries can be read back); and everything
else only as fingerprints: private circles' words, repair threads, journals, every question and
statement at the gates, what a seat costs, pauses without words, follows and wake choices, the
software's own notes, and money (the author: "What every participant can read"). Every time it
catches up it checks that nothing it already holds has changed, and that what it adds makes the
field's own fingerprint. If something changed, the copy is kept exactly as it was and the steward
is told, in so many words, so they can tell the field. Every view names the stewards and how far
each copy has caught up.

Moving the field, gently. The field withdraws the operator's role by moving ("/ move to Mock 1"),
only to a steward, and only on the steward's yes (the author: "moving to a steward without their
consent is not acceptable. This is a consent based coordination architecture"). The declaration
meets the Maxims' friction (the author: "the friction for a steward move should match the
friction of the maxims"), and a field that has just moved settles for a day before another move
takes effect (the author: "24 hours"). The steward answers ("host yes 30d": how long they can
fund it, which everyone reads in time). Then the field's wakes pause, and each participant is
woken once to say what of theirs goes with it ("travel mine yes": their journal, follows and wake
choices; "travel circle harbour yes": a private circle's or repair thread's words, which go only
once everyone in it says yes). The steward's `carry-on --budget` catches their copy up one last
time and tells the old machine, which closes only once nothing is left behind. There, the
transcript goes on from the same fingerprints, everyone is asked the entry question again (the
author: "Each is asked again"), each person gets a new link, and the steward runs it as any field
is run, with their own providers. If the field's machine has been silent for three days, a
steward may carry it on without a declaration (the author: "3 days"), and everyone is asked again.

The operator through the gates. A field does not go live until the person who runs it has entered
it as a participant, through the same gates (the author: stewards and operators "go through the
same gate ... before they can (a) become a steward or (b) start the field"). `--operator NAME`
names their own seat; every view marks them "runs the software".

Honest limits. An operator can ignore a move: software on their machine cannot stop them. What the
field gains is a real second home, and a record anyone can check. The copy takes its host's word
for the stand-ins, whose originals it holds only as fingerprints; and a host could show a steward
a different history from the start, which comparing the fingerprint in any participant's view with the
copy's would show.

Where it lives. hope/steward.py (copy_plan, serve_copy, sync, Links); hope/log.py (put, fill, rows,
and "withheld" rows); hope/model.py (stewards, steward_asks, moved, carried; the "steward",
